  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 12,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.088,
            0.65,
            0.225
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As sound wave travels away from its source, it spreads over a larger area (Fig. 10.21). Since the energy must be conserved, the same amount of energy is now spread over a larger area. Hence, the intensity decreases with distance from the source. Sounds produced with initial larger amplitude carry more energy and can travel a larger distance before intensity reduces to zero."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.658,
            0.089,
            0.885,
            0.192
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 1,
              "bbox": [
                0.658,
                0.089,
                0.885,
                0.192
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_1.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.658,
                0.198,
                0.91,
                0.228
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 10.21: Sound energy spreading over a larger area with distance"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.233,
            0.47,
            0.252
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10.6.3 Speed of Sound"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.255,
            0.89,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The speed of sound describes how fast sound waves propagate through a medium. In terms of compressions and rarefactions, one can think of speed as how fast these density disturbances propagate through the medium. The "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "speed of sound",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " can be defined as the distance which a point on a wave, such as a crest (or a trough), travels in unit time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.343,
            0.89,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For a sound wave of given frequency, the distance between two consecutive crests (or two consecutive troughs) is one wavelength ( λ ). This distance is covered by the disturbance in one time period (T ). Therefore, the speed of sound (v) is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.365,
            0.414,
            0.524,
            0.45
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_12_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{speed} = \\frac{\\mathrm{distance}}{\\mathrm{time}}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.648,
            0.416,
            0.747,
            0.451
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_12_equation_7.jpg",
          "content": "x = \\frac { \\pi } { T }"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.452,
            0.681,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We know that frequency "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\hat { \\nu } \\equiv \\frac { 1 } { T }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Eq. 10.1). Thus,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.51,
            0.486,
            0.887,
            0.503
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_12_equation_9.jpg",
          "content": "\\overline { { \\upsilon } } = \\lambda \\times \\nu\\tag{10.2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.405,
            0.506,
            0.704,
            0.525
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_12_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm { \\bar { s p e e d } = \\bar { w a v e l e n g t h } \\times \\bar { f r e q u e n c y } }"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.527,
            0.89,
            0.596
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The speed of sound depends on the medium through which it travels. It travels fastest in solids, slower in liquids, and slowest in gases. For example, sound travels about 4 – 5 times faster in water and typically 15 – 20 times faster in solids than in the air."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.598,
            0.889,
            0.667
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The speed of sound in air also depends on the temperature and humidity. As we increase the temperature or humidity, the speed of sound increases. For example, the speed of sound in dry air is about 331 m "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "S ^ { - 1 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " at "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "0 ^ { \\mathrm { ~ o ~ } } \\mathrm { C }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and nearly 344 m "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbb { S } ^ { - 1 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " at "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\bar{2}\\bar{2} ^{\\circ} C ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.022,
            0.688,
            0.08,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.022,
                0.688,
                0.08,
                0.734
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_13.jpg",
